  overview: "Quadriceps tendon rupture is an uncommon but serious failure of the knee extensor mechanism just above the patella. It is more common in people over 40 years and is sometimes associated with an underlying medical condition [r1]. The classic picture is acute pain, impaired knee extension and a suprapatellar gap. The full triad was present in only 58.3% of reported bilateral cases, so a missing feature does not exclude the injury [r2]. Its incidence is about 1.37 per 100,000 [r3].\n\nA healthy tendon rarely ruptures. In a controlled histology study of 891 spontaneously ruptured tendons, which included 82 quadriceps tendons and patellar ligaments, no ruptured tendon had a healthy structure, and 97% of the pathological changes were degenerative [r4]. The usual trigger is a low-energy event: a simple fall accounted for 61.5% of ruptures in a systematic review of repairs [r5]. The diagnosis is frequently missed or delayed [r6,r2].\n\nThe evidence behind management is observational. Surgical repair is preferred for complete ruptures, and results are worse when repair is delayed [r5], with poorer outcomes reported beyond about 3 weeks [r7]. Reviews found no clear advantage for one fixation technique [r8,r9]. Rehabilitation protocols range from cast immobilisation to early weight bearing, and the one comparative study found no clinical difference [r7,r10].",

