  returnToSport: {
    criteria: [
      "No validated return-to-sport (RTS) criteria for quadriceps tendon rupture were identified in the sources reviewed; the criteria below reflect clinical convention",
      "Full painless range of knee motion, and full active extension with no lag",
      "No effusion after running, jumping and heavy strength sessions",
      "Quadriceps strength about 90% of the other leg or of pre-injury values (clinical convention), and hop and landing symmetry about 90%",
      "Tolerates at least 2 weeks of graded sport-specific training without a flare of pain or swelling",
      "Surgeon clearance, and psychological readiness without fear of re-rupture. Expect a longer timeline: 56.8% of NFL players returned at a median of 11.3 months [r20]"
    ],
    tests: ["Isometric or isokinetic quadriceps strength with limb symmetry index", "Single, triple and crossover hop tests, and vertical jump", "Single-leg squat and step-down quality", "Effusion check (stroke test) and range of motion before and after training", "Weekly load and next-day symptom log during graded return"]
  },
  prognosis: "Outcomes after prompt repair are usually good. In a review of 12 studies, the overall re-rupture rate was 2%, most studies reported good or excellent range of motion and return to pre-injury activity, and the worst results followed delayed repair; heterotopic ossification occurred in 6.9% and DVT or pulmonary embolism in 2.5% [r5]. A later review of 6 studies reported re-rupture or failure in 0–15.4%, and a Lysholm score of 85.4–94.0 [r8]. In a 50-patient case series that included 20 quadriceps repairs and used early motion, all patients reached their pre-injury activity levels by 6 months [r22]. Return to play was 89.8% with 70.0% at the same level, and return to work was 95.9% [r21]. Professional athletes did less well: only 56.8% of NFL players returned, and they played fewer snaps in the first season back [r20]. Chronic ruptures have a less certain outlook, with failure rates of 0–33% and persistent extensor lag [r17].",
  prevention: [
    "Review medicines linked to tendon injury (fluoroquinolones, corticosteroids, testosterone therapy) with the prescriber, and choose alternatives where they exist [r14,r13]. Trial evidence that this prevents rupture is lacking.",
    "Optimise the systemic conditions linked to rupture, such as diabetes, gout, kidney disease and obesity [r1,r3,r2] (clinical reasoning; no prevention trial identified).",
    "Take new suprapatellar pain or weakness seriously and review it early, since most ruptures occur in degenerate tendons [r4].",
    "Neuromuscular, balance and proprioception training has been suggested for athletes after video analysis of rupture postures, but whether it prevents rupture is unproven [r15].",
    "Reduce fall risk in older adults, since simple falls account for most repaired ruptures [r5] (clinical reasoning)."
  ],
  clinicalPearls: [
    "Inability to straight-leg raise after a knee injury is a quadriceps or patellar tendon rupture until proved otherwise. Refer urgently and do not start exercises first [r7].",
    "Do not wait for the full triad: pain, impaired extension and a suprapatellar gap were all present in only 58.3% of reported bilateral cases, and diagnosis was delayed in 30.5% of bilateral cases [r2].",
    "Check the other knee. Bilateral ruptures are reported, with diabetes, gout, steroid use and obesity among the associations [r1,r2].",
    "A patella spur on a knee radiograph is common in ruptured knees (62% against 19% of controls), so look at the tendon when you see one [r6].",
    "Surgery is better early, but rehabilitation need not be long in a cast. Early functional protocols with full weight bearing and splint use were not worse in the only comparison, and both retears in one series came from not wearing the splint [r10,r18].",
    "Set honest expectations for athletes. Return to play was high in a systematic review (89.8%) but lower in professional athletes (70.9% in the review and 56.8% in the NFL), with a median of 11.3 months in the NFL [r21,r20]."
  ],
